  "dom_id": "step-q350679",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "What is the sum of the series $1-\\frac{{(5\\pi /3)}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{(5\\pi /3)}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,(5\\pi /3)^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯\\,?$\n\nThe sum of the series is\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "We recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{x}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}) & =1-\\frac{{(5\\pi /3)}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{(5\\pi /3)}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,(5\\pi /3)^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯ \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =1-\\frac{{(5\\pi /3)}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{(5\\pi /3)}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,(5\\pi /3)^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $\\frac{1}{2}.$",
